PRE-TABLE BLOCK -- #494 wdcaltoffset
Written 2026-08-30 BEFORE the product edit and BEFORE table 1, so this file's own
mtime proves the order.  Recorded OUTPUT, not assertion.

SCOPE: this round extends a kill-table-backed detector, so the block is MANDATORY.

====================================================================================
1.  SIBLING PARITY
====================================================================================

The diff does not de-fatalise; it adds an OFFSET DECODE to a handler that had none.
The sibling arms are therefore the two offsets of one region, and the parity question
is whether the ACCEPT arm (offset 0) and the DECLINE arm (offset 1) are observable
through the same channels.

ASYMMETRY FOUND, and it is the round's main finding:

  offset 0 (accept)   observable through DRQ, the input ring, AND the ATAPI
                      signature (cyl_lo/cyl_hi), because all three are touched
  offset 1 (decline)  observable through the SAME three -- but the shipped 15-row
                      witness reads only the FIRST TWO

  >  The reset action has TWO effects and the rows observe ONE of them.
  >  `d->cur_command = COMMAND_RESET;` is assigned by the CALLER; the ring and DRQ
  >  are cleared by `wdc_new_command_reset()`.  A fix guarding only the helper call
  >  leaves the signature latch standing and every existing row stays green.

ROW OWED: the ATAPI-signature row.  MEASURED ALREADY as escaping -- see CHANNEL/M3.

====================================================================================
2.  CLAIM -> ROW  (semantic trigger: does the sentence assert something a row could
    observe?  Claim VERBS are examples, not the test -- a "would <consequence>"
    counterfactual qualifies.)
====================================================================================

Added comment sentences that assert an observable consequence:

  C1  "offset 1 is the Drive Address register and is READ-ONLY"
        -> ROW: `defect 0x3f7 store must not abort the write` (exists, P2)
  C2  "declining it leaves a live transfer untouched"
        -> ROW: `defect 0x3f7 store must not purge staged bytes` (exists, P2)
  C3  "a legal 0x3f6 store must still reset"
        -> ROW: P1 accept (exists), and MEASURED failing under m1 over-reach
  C4  "the decline covers the signature latch, not only the helper"
        -> ROW: *** OWED -- the ATAPI-signature row.  This is the sentence that had
           no implementation, and m3 is the mutant that proves it. ***
  C5  "the decline returns 1 (handled), so the access never reaches unassigned-
       address handling"
        -> NO ROW, and the reason is recorded rather than left blank: the round
           MEASURED the alternative (m4, width 1) and found it green on every row,
           i.e. the rows cannot distinguish handled-and-declined from unmapped.
           A row here would be vacuous by construction.  The claim is pinned by
           READING (`return 1` is on the common tail, not an early return) and by
           m4's measured warning flood, not by an oracle.

====================================================================================
3.  GUARD FIELD vs BODY FIELD
====================================================================================

PREDICATE reads:   relative_addr   (an access parameter, not device state)
                   idata           (the stored byte, via `idata & WDCTL_RST`)

GUARDED BODY touches:
                   d->cur_command                 <- WRITTEN by the caller
                   wdc_new_command_reset(d) ->    d->inbuf_head, d->inbuf_tail,
                                                  d->write_in_progress,
                                                  d->write_count,
                                                  d->atapi_cmd_in_progress, ...

WHERE THEY DIFFER: the predicate reads NO device field that the body writes, so the
classic disagreeing-state form does not arise -- `relative_addr` cannot be desynced
from anything because it is recomputed per access.

  >  BUT THE CHECK STILL PAYS, BY ITS OTHER HALF: the body's writes SPLIT across two
  >  sites with different owners (caller vs helper), and the rows cover only the
  >  helper's.  That is the same shape as a guard field and a body field written at
  >  different sites, one level up -- effects rather than fields.

N/A discharged with reason: no third-state row is owed for a field pair, because
there is no field pair.  The row owed is the split-effect row (C4).

